-- statement:
--   For n≥0, rooted planar maps with n+1 edges are encoded by two permutations a and s of 2(n+1) darts: a is a fixed-point-free involution, the graph on s-orbits joined by a is connected, and the numbers of s-orbits and sa-orbits sum to n+3. Maps are identified under simultaneous dart relabellings fixing dart 0; vertices and edges are the s-orbits and a-orbits of a chosen representative. Loops and multiple edges are allowed. For every edge subset A, k(A) counts the connected components of its spanning graph, including isolated vertices, and its Fortuin–Kasteleyn weight is q^{k(A)+(|A|−|V|)/2}. Summing these weights over all rooted maps and all A gives the partition function and hence the normalized finite expectation. The metric-measure space associated to a map has scaled full-graph distance and degree measure, obtained by assigning each dart mass 1/[2(n+1)] and pushing this to its incident vertex; loops contribute two darts. Root and edge decoration are discarded from this space. General metric-measure data specify a carrier, real-valued distance, measurable structure and measure; validity means these form a compact metric space with its Borel probability measure. Their Gromov–Hausdorff–Prokhorov extended distance is the infimum, over pseudometrics on their disjoint union extending both distances, of the maximum of the Hausdorff distance between the two images and the Lévy–Prokhorov distance between their pushed-forward measures. Test functions are real-valued, globally bounded and continuous for this distance on valid data. For e:[0,1]→ℝ, set d_e(s,t)=e(s)+e(t)−2 inf_{u∈[min(s,t),max(s,t)]}e(u); the associated tree presentation quotients by the equivalence relation generated by d_e=0, uses chosen representatives for its distance, and carries the pushforward of uniform time. Three independent standard real Brownian motions define the normalized excursion e(t)=√(Σᵢ(Bᵢ(t)−tBᵢ(1))²). MainStatement is the defined proposition that, for every q>4, some c>0 makes the finite expectations of every such test function on maps with distances multiplied by c/√(n+1) converge, as n→∞ through all nonnegative integers, to its expectation on this excursion tree. This is required for every probability space and every realization of the three independent Brownian motions.
